# Secrets — never commit these.
# AI provider keys are not in any of these: they live in the OS credential
# store (Windows Credential Manager / Keychain / Secret Service). The only
# exception is the opt-in unprotected fallback, which writes
# credentials/provider-keys.json — covered by credentials/ below.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
credentials/
client_secret.json
client_secret*.json

# Written by build.ps1 from .env and bundled into the .exe. Only the Supabase
# anon key, which is public by design -- but it is generated output, not
# source, and it is per-deployment.
supabase.json

# Provider choices. No keys in it, but it is per-user state, not source.
providers.json

# Build output
build/
dist/
*.spec

# Files the agent downloads or writes
workspace/

# Local run output
agent.log
activity.log
activity.log.*
crash.log

# Chromium's profile for the browser tools — cookies and live logins
browser-profile/

# The reminders database — the user's own data, not the app's
reminders.db
reminders.db-wal
reminders.db-shm

# Python
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/

# Lint cache
.ruff_cache/

# Server secrets. The API server reads its configuration from the environment
# in production and from .env in a checkout; .env is already ignored above, and
# these are the extra shapes a Supabase/Postgres setup tends to leave lying
# around.
supabase/.temp/
supabase/.branches/
*.dump
*.sql.gz
